3><STRUCT_0004>\nThus it suffices to show the lemma in case <MATH_0021> and <MATH_0022> are affine.\nSay <MATH_0023> and <MATH_0024> with <MATH_0025> locally nilpotent.\nBy Algebra, Lemma <REF_0004> we know that\n<MATH_0026> is the spectrum of a ring <MATH_0027> with\n<MATH_0028>\nsuch that\n<MATH_0029>\nmaps to an invertible element in <MATH_0030>. Choose any lifts\n<MATH_0031>. Set\n<MATH_0032>\nSince <MATH_0033> is locally nilpotent the ideal <MATH_0034> is locally nilpotent\n(Algebra, Lemma <REF_0005>).\nObserve that <MATH_0035>.\nIt follows that the determinant of the matrix of partials of the\n<MATH_0036> is invertible in the algebra <MATH_0037> by\nAlgebra, Lemma <REF_0006>.\nHence <MATH_0038> is <ACCENT_0004>tale and the proof is complete.\n", "source_text_hash": "sha256:93577a39eb4fdd278ab88aeae7f745b92c4fb1fdb19b4290e4bc49ab5f56c0c5", "unit_id": "tag:039R:proof"}
